# dotfiles-MacBook/.gitignore — config + scripts only; never machine state/secrets.
# ROOT-ANCHORED (leading slash) on purpose: a bare `.claude/` matches at EVERY depth, so it
# also shadowed the VENDORED core/.claude/ tree (agents/, commands/, hooks/, settings.json).
# Those files stayed visible only because they were tracked before the rule existed — a
# future Core release adding one there would have been invisible to `git status` and needed
# `git add -f` during a subtree conflict. Keep the slash.
/.claude/
# machine-specific shell overrides (never synced; sourced last by .zshrc)
zsh/local.zsh
# Core is a vendored git-subtree under core/ and IS tracked — including the nvim
# lazy-lock.json, which is owned by dotfiles-core so every machine pins the same
# plugin set. Bump it upstream (make update-nvim-plugins) and re-sync, never here.
# noise / secrets
*.log
*.bak
.DS_Store
# Crash dumps. `core.[0-9]*` and NOT `core.*`: this repo tracks core.lock, and Core itself
# tracks core.manifest and core.version — a blanket rule would hide the next such file the
# moment someone added one, silently. Bare `core` is deliberately not matched either: that
# is the vendored Core DIRECTORY. Matches the kernel/glibc `core.<pid>` spelling.
core.[0-9]*
# editor swap/temp files (vim/neovim drop these next to the file being edited)
*.sw[a-p]
*~
.*.un~
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# private SSH material — deny-by-default, with a NAMED allowlist. Never invert this to
# an ignore-list of key patterns: `ssh/*` means a key dropped here is untracked unless
# someone deliberately names it below, which is the safe direction to be wrong in.
ssh/*
# ssh/os.conf → ~/.ssh/config.d/50-os.conf via Core's blib_link_os_layer. Carries this
# host's per-service key NAMES (never key material) so they survive Core owning the
# portable ssh config (dotgibson/dotfiles-core#450).
!ssh/os.conf
# ssh/hosts.conf.example → SEEDED (copied, never symlinked) to
# ~/.ssh/config.d/10-hosts.conf on first bootstrap. Placeholders only: the seed is
# tracked and public, the seeded copy in $HOME is untracked and carries the real
# hosts. Adding this name does NOT widen `ssh/*` — a key dropped here is still
# untracked unless it, too, is named above.
!ssh/hosts.conf.example
